---
import LegalLayout from "../layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
import { LEGAL, PAGES } from "../legal";
---

<LegalLayout
  lang="en"
  page="privacy"
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="What Snap2Link does with your data: the website, the app, Google Drive and Snap2Link hosting."
>
  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Snap2Link has no accounts, no tracking, no analytics and no advertising.</li>
    <li>The app runs on your computer. Your settings stay there.</li>
    <li>With Google Drive, captures go to your own Drive and never pass through us.</li>
    <li>With Snap2Link hosting, a capture is kept for 6 hours at most.</li>
    <li>We never store your IP address, and we collect nothing about your device.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
  <p>
    The controller of the data described here is {LEGAL.publisher}, {LEGAL.address}. For any
    question, write to {LEGAL.contact}.
  </p>

  <h2>The website</h2>
  <p>
    snap2link.app sets no cookies of its own and has no analytics. Cloudflare delivers the site
    and processes your IP address to do so and to protect it. On the home page, your browser asks
    GitHub for the number of stars and downloads, so GitHub sees your IP address.
  </p>

  <h2>The app on your computer</h2>
  <p>
    Settings, shortcuts and tokens are stored on your computer. The app sends us no telemetry. It
    checks GitHub for updates, so GitHub sees your IP address. Captures are temporary files,
    removed after they are sent or the next time the app starts.
  </p>

  <h2>Google Drive</h2>
  <p>
    If you choose Google Drive, the app connects directly to your Google account. It asks for two
    permissions: access to the files it creates (<code>drive.file</code>), and your email address,
    shown in the settings. Your Google token stays on your computer. Captures go to your Drive
    with a public link, and we never receive them. Google's privacy policy applies.
  </p>
  <p>
    Snap2Link's use and transfer of information received from Google APIs adheres to the
    <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy"
      >Google API Services User Data Policy</a
    >, including the Limited Use requirements.
  </p>

  <h2>Snap2Link hosting</h2>
  <p>If you choose Snap2Link hosting, which needs no account, we keep the following.</p>
  <div class="legal-table">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Data</th><th scope="col">Why</th><th scope="col">How long</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>The capture, its link, size and dates</td>
          <td>To give you a link (performance of the service)</td>
          <td>
            The link stops at 6 hours, or when you press Delete. The data is erased at the next
            cleanup, which runs every 30 minutes.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A code that lets you delete the capture, stored as a hash</td>
          <td>So that only you can delete it (performance of the service)</td>
          <td>Same as the capture</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A random guest token, stored as a hash</td>
          <td>To apply daily limits and stop abuse (legitimate interest)</td>
          <td>90 days. Your copy stays on your computer.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Which guest sent a capture</td>
          <td>To act on a report (legitimate interest)</td>
          <td>Same as the capture</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Daily counters, tied to a secret hash of your address block</td>
          <td>To apply daily limits (legitimate interest)</td>
          <td>Until the next cleanup after midnight UTC. The hash changes every day.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Anyone who has the link can see the capture. We don't look at captures, unless one is reported
    to us or the law requires it.
  </p>

  <h2>The security check</h2>
  <p>
    Before your first upload to Snap2Link hosting, and when your guest token expires, a window
    runs Cloudflare Turnstile to tell people from bots. It usually needs no click. Turnstile
    processes signals such as your IP address, TLS fingerprint and browser user agent, not to
    identify you but to detect bots. Our legitimate interest is to protect the service from abuse.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cloudflare acts as our processor for this check, and as a controller when it uses these
    signals to improve Turnstile. See the
    <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/turnstile-privacy-policy/">Turnstile Privacy Addendum</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Reports</h2>
  <p>
    If you report content, we keep your message and contact details to handle it and to keep proof
    of what we did, for up to one year.
  </p>

  <h2>The Claude Code plugin</h2>
  <p>
    The plugin runs on your machine and downloads the capture from its link. It sends us nothing.
    What you do with a capture in an AI tool is covered by that tool's own policy.
  </p>

  <h2>Who else handles data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare</a>: website, Snap2Link
      hosting, security check.
    </li>
    <li><a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google</a>: only if you choose Drive.</li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement"
        >GitHub</a
      >: downloads, updates, and the counters on the home page.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These companies are based in the United States and may process data outside the European
    Union, under the safeguards described in their policies. We sell no data.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You can ask to access, correct or delete your data, to restrict its use, to object to it, and
    to receive it in a portable format. Write to {LEGAL.contact}. Because there are no accounts, we
    usually can't connect data to you unless you give us the link concerned. You can also
    complain to {LEGAL.authority}.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes, the date at the top changes too. See also the
    <a href={PAGES.en.terms}>terms</a> and the <a href={PAGES.en.legal}>legal notice</a>.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
